### ============================================================
### Phase 1 manual checks
### Environment: select "dev" in the HTTP Client env selector.
### Secrets come from http-client.private.env.json (gitignored).
### ============================================================

### 1. User signup
### Expected: 201 Created.
### In dev, the 6-digit OTP is printed in the backend log:
###   [dev] OTP for <email> (signup) is: 123456
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/auth/signup
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "email": "{{userEmail}}",
  "name": "{{userName}}",
  "password": "{{userPassword}}"
}

### 2. Verify signup OTP
### Replace the OTP below with the one from the backend log.
### Expected: 200 OK, data.token present.
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/auth/verify-otp
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "email": "{{userEmail}}",
  "otp": "REPLACE_WITH_6_DIGIT_OTP"
}

> {%
    client.global.set("userToken", response.body.data.token);
    client.global.set("userId", response.body.data.userId);
%}

### 3. User login
### Expected: 200 OK, data.token present.
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/auth/login
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "email": "{{userEmail}}",
  "password": "{{userPassword}}"
}

> {%
    client.global.set("userToken", response.body.data.token);
    client.global.set("userId", response.body.data.userId);
%}

### 4. Admin login
### Expected: 200 OK, data.token and data.role present.
### Requires APP_ADMIN_USERNAME / APP_ADMIN_PASSWORD in the backend Run Configuration,
### and an empty admins table on first startup so AdminBootstrap creates the account.
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/admin/auth/login
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "usernameOrEmail": "{{adminUsername}}",
  "password": "{{adminPassword}}"
}

> {%
    client.global.set("adminToken", response.body.data.token);
%}

### 5. Admin route with NO token
### Expected: 401 Unauthorized (JSON "Authentication required").
GET {{baseUrl}}/api/admin/ping

### 6. Admin route with a USER token
### Expected: 401 Unauthorized (user token is signed with the user secret).
GET {{baseUrl}}/api/admin/ping
Authorization: Bearer {{userToken}}

### 7. Admin route with an ADMIN token
### Expected: 404 Not Found. There is no /api/admin/ping handler, but reaching 404
### proves the admin token passed authentication and authorization.
GET {{baseUrl}}/api/admin/ping
Authorization: Bearer {{adminToken}}

### 8. ADMIN token on a user route
### Expected: 401 Unauthorized (admin token is signed with the admin secret).
GET {{baseUrl}}/api/saved-posts
Authorization: Bearer {{adminToken}}

### 9. Protected user route with a USER token
### Expected: 200 OK.
GET {{baseUrl}}/api/saved-posts
Authorization: Bearer {{userToken}}

### 10. Rate limit
### Auth routes allow 20 requests/minute per IP.
### Run this block repeatedly. The 21st request in the same minute should be:
###   429 Too Many Requests + Retry-After header.
### Earlier auth requests in this file also count toward the same limit.
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/auth/login
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "email": "{{userEmail}}",
  "password": "wrong-password"
}

### 11. X-Request-ID echo
### Expected: 200 OK, and the response header X-Request-ID is exactly test-12345678.
GET {{baseUrl}}/api/saved-posts
Authorization: Bearer {{userToken}}
X-Request-ID: test-12345678

### 12. Invalid X-Request-ID is replaced
### Expected: 200 OK, and X-Request-ID is a generated 32-character hex value
### (the incoming "bad" is too short).
GET {{baseUrl}}/api/saved-posts
Authorization: Bearer {{userToken}}
X-Request-ID: bad

### 13. Blocked-user flow
### 1) In IntelliJ Database tool window run:
###      UPDATE users SET status = 'PERM_BLOCKED' WHERE email = '{{userEmail}}';
### 2) JWTfilter caches user:status:<userId> in Redis for 1 hour. Restart the Redis
###    container from IntelliJ's Services/Docker window (or delete the key) so the
###    change is visible immediately.
### 3) Run this request.
### Expected: 403 Forbidden, message contains "permanently suspended".
GET {{baseUrl}}/api/saved-posts
Authorization: Bearer {{userToken}}

### 14. Restore the user after the blocked-user check
### In the IntelliJ Database tool window run:
###      UPDATE users SET status = 'ACTIVE' WHERE email = '{{userEmail}}';
### Then restart Redis again to clear the cached PERM_BLOCKED status.
